            {
              "id": "5d7edaa1-2226-3798-bf2c-e415bee7b8bb",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of functions like `rand()` to generate random numbers. These functions are not suitable for generating random numbers in a security context because they are predictable and can be easily reproduced. This can lead to various security issues, such as predictability in password generation, session identifiers, or cryptographic keys.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a strong cryptographic random number generator. In C++, you can use functions provided by libraries like `<random>` which are designed to produce high-quality random numbers suitable for cryptographic use.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `rand()` function with a strong cryptographic random number generator. Here is an example of how you can do this in C++:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_int_distribution<> dis(-3, 3);\n\nhc[i] = CAST(dis(gen));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<random>`: This library is used to generate high-quality random numbers.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264",
                  "5d7edaa1-2226-3798-bf2c-e415bee7b8bb"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0b94e0ed-efe0-3a6d-acb7-852aae04f801",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of functions like `rand()` to generate random numbers. These functions are not suitable for generating random numbers in a security context because they are predictable and can be easily reproduced. This can lead to various security issues, such as predictability in password generation, session identifiers, or cryptographic keys.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a strong cryptographic random number generator. In C++, you can use functions provided by libraries like `<random>` which are designed to produce high-quality random numbers suitable for cryptographic use.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `rand()` function with a strong cryptographic random number generator. Here is an example of how you can do this in C++:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_int_distribution<> dis(-3, 3);\n\nhc[i] = CAST(dis(gen));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<random>`: This library is used to generate high-quality random numbers.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
