              "id": "a7f0ddb7-c478-3e4d-8fad-82f795cd3d89",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `atof()` function is used, which converts a string to a double. This function does not perform any error checking, and if the input string is not a valid representation of a double, it can lead to undefined behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using C library functions that do not perform error checking or that can lead to buffer overflows. Instead, use safer alternatives that are available in the C++ Standard Library. For example, instead of using `atof()`, you can use `std::stod()` which throws an exception if the conversion cannot be performed.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `atof()` function with `std::stod()`. Here is the fixed code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\n// ...\n\ntry {\n    beta = std::stod(argv[++i]);\n} catch (const std::invalid_argument& ia) {\n    // Handle the exception\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: for `std::atof()`\n- `<string>`: for `std::stod()`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
