              "id": "84fd0ca6-f266-31dc-958b-ce72e13fc8e8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of functions like `rand()` to generate random numbers. These functions are not suitable for generating random numbers in a security context because they are predictable and can be easily reproduced. This can lead to various security issues, such as predictability in password generation, session identifiers, or cryptographic keys.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a strong cryptographic random number generator. In C++, you can use functions provided by libraries like `<random>` which are designed to produce high-quality random numbers suitable for cryptographic use.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `rand()` function with a strong cryptographic random number generator. Here is an example of how you can do this in C++:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_int_distribution<> dis(-3, 3);\n\nhb[i] = CAST(dis(gen));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak Pseudo-Random Number Generator (PRNG)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264",
                  "84fd0ca6-f266-31dc-958b-ce72e13fc8e8"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "778a2dc6-36f3-36a4-aa73-4e5a4e4efb2c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `atof` function is used, which converts a string to a double. This function does not check for overflow or underflow, and does not report any errors if the string does not represent a valid number.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using C library functions that do not perform bounds checking or error reporting. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. In this case, you can use `std::stod` function which throws an exception if the conversion cannot be performed.\n\n## Source Code Fix\n\nReplace the `atof` function with `std::stod`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n\ntry {\n    alpha = std::stod(argv[++i]);\n} catch (const std::invalid_argument& ia) {\n    // Handle the exception\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<string>`: This is a standard C++ library that provides support for strings.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
