              "id": "f8ef7428-887a-3d18-9130-f9ceccf55606",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `atoi()` function is used, which is a standard C library function that converts a string to an integer. The problem with `atoi()` is that it does not handle errors properly. If the input string cannot be converted into an integer, it returns zero, which can lead to unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `atoi()`. Instead, use functions that can handle errors properly. In C++, you can use `std::stoi()`, which throws an exception if the conversion fails. Always validate and sanitize user input to prevent injection attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `atoi()` with `std::stoi()`. Here is the fixed code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n\ntry {\n    ldc = std::stoi(argv[++i]);\n} catch (std::invalid_argument& e) {\n    // handle error\n} catch (std::out_of_range& e) {\n    // handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<string>`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Proactive Controls](https://owasp.org/www-project-proactive-controls/)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-20: Improper Input Validation](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html)\n- [CWE-807: Reliance on Untrusted Inputs in a Security Decision](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/807.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
