            {
              "id": "2191df6a-6c2c-3597-b162-b9d6bf369e21",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that arises when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe. In this case, the function `atoi()` is used, which is known to be unsafe because it does not check for overflow or underflow errors and does not handle errors properly.\n\nThe `atoi()` function converts a string to an integer. If the string cannot be converted into an integer, it returns zero. This can lead to unexpected behavior if the string is not a valid integer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer alternatives to `atoi()`. In C++, you can use `std::stoi()`, which throws an exception if the conversion fails. This allows you to handle errors properly.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `atoi()` function with `std::stoi()`. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\ntry {\n    ldb = std::stoi(argv[++i]);\n} catch (std::invalid_argument const &e) {\n    std::cout << \"Bad input: std::invalid_argument thrown\" << '\\n';\n} catch (std::out_of_range const &e) {\n    std::cout << \"Integer overflow: std::out_of_range thrown\" << '\\n';\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: This library is required for `atoi()`.\n- `<string>`: This library is required for `std::stoi()`.\n- `<iostream>`: This library is required for `std::cout`.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
